(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-neuro-exam-quiz"]={"title":"Neurological Exam (Central & Peripheral Nervous System) — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which is one of the six categories of the neurological examination?","choices":["Cranial nerves","Blood pressure","Heart auscultation","Abdominal percussion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The six categories of the neuro exam are mental status, cranial nerves, motor system, reflexes, sensory system, and coordination/station/gait. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"When is the mental status typically assessed during the examination?","choices":["During the initial interview","Only after the reflex testing","Only during the sensory exam","Only while assessing the gait"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mental status is typically obtained during the initial interview, while cranial nerves are assessed with the head and neck exam. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"A patient who appears drowsy, opens the eyes and looks at you, but falls asleep when not stimulated is described as what?","choices":["Alert","Lethargic","Stuporous","Comatose"],"answer":1,"exp":"A lethargic patient appears drowsy, opens the eyes and looks at the examiner, and responds to questions but then falls asleep. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient who arouses only after painful stimuli and lapses back to unresponsiveness when the stimulus stops is in what state?","choices":["Alertness","Stupor","Lethargy","Obtundation"],"answer":1,"exp":"In stupor, the patient arouses from sleep only after painful stimuli, gives slow or absent verbal responses, and lapses back into unresponsiveness when the stimulus ceases. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which term describes a patient who is unarousable, with eyes closed and no response even to painful stimuli?","choices":["Stupor","Lethargy","Obtundation","Coma"],"answer":3,"exp":"A comatose patient is unarousable with eyes closed and shows no response to painful stimuli. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient oriented to person, place, and time is documented as oriented to what degree?","choices":["Oriented times one","Oriented times four","Oriented times five","Oriented times three"],"answer":3,"exp":"A patient oriented to person, place, and time is documented as oriented times three; adding event makes it times four. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Adding awareness of the event or circumstances of the illness to person, place, and time makes a patient oriented to what degree?","choices":["Oriented times two","Oriented times three","Oriented times six","Oriented times four"],"answer":3,"exp":"Orientation to person, place, time, and event is documented as oriented times four. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Serial sevens and spelling the word WORLD backwards are tests of what?","choices":["Cranial nerve function","Calculation and cognitive function","Deep tendon reflexes","Peripheral vibratory sense"],"answer":1,"exp":"Serial sevens, digit span, and spelling WORLD backwards test calculation and cognitive function during the mental status exam. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"Evaluating a patient for aphasia is part of assessing which mental status component?","choices":["Muscle tone","Coordination","Pupillary reflex","Language"],"answer":3,"exp":"Evaluating for aphasia assesses language, including word comprehension, repetition, naming, reading comprehension, and writing. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 128 Hertz tuning fork is used in the neuro exam to test what?","choices":["Vibratory sense","Visual acuity","Deep tendon reflexes","Sense of smell"],"answer":0,"exp":"A 128 Hertz tuning fork is used to test vibratory sense, whereas a 512 Hertz fork is used for the Rinne and Weber hearing tests. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which tuning fork is used for the Rinne and Weber hearing tests?","choices":["A 128 Hertz tuning fork","A 60 Hertz tuning fork","A 512 Hertz tuning fork","A 1000 Hertz tuning fork"],"answer":2,"exp":"A 512 Hertz tuning fork is used for the Rinne and Weber hearing tests, while a 128 Hertz fork tests vibratory sense. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"Cranial nerve one, the olfactory nerve, is tested by assessing what?","choices":["The sense of vision","The sense of smell","The sense of hearing","The sense of taste"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cranial nerve one, the olfactory nerve, is a sensory nerve tested by assessing the sense of smell in each nostril with a familiar aroma such as coffee or cinnamon. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"When testing the olfactory nerve, which type of substance should be avoided?","choices":["Pleasant familiar aromas like coffee","Any substance the patient recognizes","Substances tested with eyes closed","Irritating substances such as menthol or ammonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"When testing smell, irritating substances such as menthol or ammonia should be avoided; a pleasant familiar aroma like coffee or cinnamon is preferred. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Cranial nerve two, the optic nerve, is tested by which of the following?","choices":["Testing the sense of smell","Palpating the masseter muscle","Having the patient shrug the shoulders","Testing visual acuity with an eye chart"],"answer":3,"exp":"The optic nerve is tested through visual acuity with an eye chart, inspection of the optic fundi, visual fields by confrontation, and the pupillary light reflex. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"During the optic nerve exam, what must be done before shining a light in the patient's eyes?","choices":["The gag reflex must be tested first","Visual acuity must be tested first","The reflexes must be graded first","The tongue must be inspected first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Visual acuity must be tested before shining a light in the patient's eyes, because bright light can transiently affect the acuity assessment. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"The abbreviation PERRL, used with cranial nerve three, stands for what?","choices":["Painless extraocular rotation, right and left","Pupils equal, round, and reactive to light","Position, extension, resistance, and reflex level","Palpate each rib, then reassess the lungs"],"answer":1,"exp":"PERRL stands for pupils equal, round, and reactive to light, documenting the pupillary exam related to the oculomotor nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve carries the motor (efferent) limb of the pupillary light reflex, constricting the pupil?","choices":["The oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve three)","The optic nerve (cranial nerve two)","The trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four)","The abducens nerve (cranial nerve six)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve, cranial nerve three, carries the parasympathetic motor limb of the pupillary light reflex and constricts the pupil. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"By the mnemonic 'SO4, LR6, all the rest are 3,' the lateral rectus muscle is innervated by which nerve?","choices":["The trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four)","The oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve three)","The trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve five)","The abducens nerve (cranial nerve six)"],"answer":3,"exp":"By the mnemonic SO4, LR6, all the rest are 3, the lateral rectus is innervated by the abducens nerve (cranial nerve six). — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which single eye muscle is innervated by the trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four)?","choices":["The lateral rectus","The medial rectus","The superior oblique","The inferior rectus"],"answer":2,"exp":"By the mnemonic SO4, the superior oblique is innervated by the trochlear nerve (cranial nerve four). — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"The trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve five) provides motor innervation to which muscles?","choices":["The muscles of mastication (temporalis and masseter)","The muscles of facial expression","The intrinsic muscles of the tongue","The sternocleidomastoid and trapezius"],"answer":0,"exp":"The trigeminal nerve provides motor innervation to the muscles of mastication, the temporalis and masseter, and sensation to the face through its three divisions. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is the sensory function of the trigeminal nerve tested?","choices":["By asking the patient to smell coffee","By having the patient say the word Ah","By testing light touch in all three facial divisions","By checking the patient's grip strength"],"answer":2,"exp":"Trigeminal sensory function is tested by applying light touch (and pain) with a cotton wisp or swab in all three divisions — ophthalmic, maxillary, and mandibular. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"The facial nerve (cranial nerve seven) is tested for motor function by which maneuver?","choices":["Shrugging the shoulders against resistance","Raising the eyebrows and closing the eyes tightly","Protruding the tongue to each side","Clenching the teeth while palpating the jaw"],"answer":1,"exp":"The facial nerve is tested by assessing facial symmetry and having the patient raise the eyebrows, close the eyes tightly, smile or show teeth, and puff out the cheeks. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (cranial nerve eight) is responsible for which two functions?","choices":["Smell and taste","Vision and eye movement","Balance and hearing","Chewing and facial expression"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vestibulocochlear nerve, cranial nerve eight, is a sensory nerve responsible for balance (vestibular) and hearing (cochlear). — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"Having the patient say 'Ah' and observing the soft palate and uvula tests which cranial nerves?","choices":["The olfactory and optic nerves (one and two)","The trochlear and abducens nerves (four and six)","The glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves (nine and ten)","The accessory and hypoglossal nerves (eleven and twelve)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Saying Ah to observe symmetric rise of the soft palate and midline uvula, plus the gag reflex and swallowing, tests the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves together. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"The spinal accessory nerve (cranial nerve eleven) is tested by which maneuver?","choices":["Raising the eyebrows and smiling widely","Shrugging the shoulders and turning the head against resistance","Protruding the tongue and moving it sideways","Clenching the teeth and puffing the cheeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"The spinal accessory nerve is tested by having the patient shrug the shoulders (trapezius) and turn the head (sternocleidomastoid) against resistance. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 31"},{"q":"The hypoglossal nerve (cranial nerve twelve) is tested by asking the patient to do what?","choices":["Protrude the tongue and move it side to side","Shrug the shoulders upward strongly","Follow a finger through the cardinal gazes","Identify a familiar odor with eyes closed"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hypoglossal nerve, which innervates the intrinsic tongue muscles, is tested by having the patient protrude the tongue and move it side to side, watching for deviation, atrophy, or fasciculations. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which structures make up the central nervous system?","choices":["The brain and spinal cord","The cranial nerves and spinal nerves","The dermatomes and peripheral nerves","The muscles and tendons of the limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"The central nervous system is made up of the brain and the spinal cord, while the cranial and spinal nerves make up the peripheral nervous system. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"How many pairs of spinal nerves are part of the peripheral nervous system?","choices":["12 pairs","24 pairs","31 pairs","40 pairs"],"answer":2,"exp":"The peripheral nervous system includes the twelve cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves, each with an anterior motor root and a posterior sensory root. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Besides constricting the pupil, the oculomotor nerve elevates the upper eyelid by innervating which muscle?","choices":["The orbicularis oculi muscle","The superior oblique muscle","The levator palpebrae superioris","The lateral rectus muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve elevates the upper eyelid by innervating the levator palpebrae superioris, in addition to constricting the pupil and moving most eye muscles. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Interpreting a proverb such as 'you can't judge a book by its cover' tests which mental status function?","choices":["Abstract reasoning","Peripheral vibratory sense","Deep tendon reflex activity","Hand grip strength testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Interpreting proverbs and solving problems tests abstract reasoning during the mental status exam. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 15"}]};
